<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I Doukas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1240–1289</span> · Also known as John I Doukas of Thessaly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I Doukas was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> from about 1268 until his death, an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II of Epirus</a>. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> with the Byzantine title of sebastokrator, he resisted Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, defeating a Byzantine army near Neopatras around 1275, and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. His sons Constantine and John II succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-doukas.jpg" alt="John I Doukas" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Кардам · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ioannes_Doukas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
